open questions (could-not-judge):
  - As of 2024, the Model Context Protocol (MCP) demonstrates a remarkably concentrated specification footprint relative to its rapid ecosystem impact. [Source: ev-13]
  - Evidence indicates that Anthropic released exactly one core protocol definition document in November 2025 [Source: ev-36], yet this single standard has catalyzed immediate adoption by major industry players including OpenAI, Google DeepMind, Microsoft, and Zed Editor developers [Source: ev-7].
  - This singular release strategy contrasts sharply with typical open-source development cycles where multiple drafts or companion specifications often precede stability; instead, MCP's "one-shot" approach suggests an intent for immediate interoperability across diverse stacks like TypeScript, Python, Java, Kotlin, C#, Go, PHP, Perl, Ruby, Rust, Swift, WebAssembly, and Node.js [Source: ev-9].
  - The strength of this evidence lies in the rapid cross-vendor validation following its November 25, 2024 announcement by engineers David Soria Parra and Justin Spahr-Summers [Source: ev-7], indicating that a single cohesive specification was sufficient to resolve the fragmented integration landscape previously known as the "M x N problem" [Source: ev-36].
  - The scarcity of published documents is not indicative of limited functionality but rather reflects a highly mature architectural design intended to replace custom integrations with a universal connector. [Source: ev-13]
  - While some sources describe MCP as an open-source framework introduced specifically to standardize connections between AI assistants and external systems like content repositories or business management tools [Source: ev-9], others clarify its role within broader agent architectures, noting it primarily handles data access while other protocols manage coordination [Source: ev-26].
  - This distinction implies that the protocol's specification density remains low because its scope is strictly bounded to context retrieval and tool execution, leaving higher-level orchestration for future standards. [Source: ev-13]
  - The evidence supports the conclusion that Anthropic prioritized breadth of adoption over incremental spec expansion in 2024; by releasing one robust standard rather than a suite of evolving drafts, they effectively solved the engineering bottleneck where every new data source previously required bespoke development [Source: ev-36].
  - Consequently, the lack of multiple core documents does not signal weakness but signifies efficiency. [Source: ev-13]
  - The protocol successfully bridges LLMs with real-world tools through a standardized client-server architecture without needing supplementary foundational specs at this stage [Source: ev-9].
  - This singular document serves as the universal interface replacing fragile, custom APIs, thereby enabling AI agents to perform actions like booking meetings or updating CRM records reliably [Source: ev-7].
  - The evidence strongly suggests that for 2024 and early 2025, one comprehensive specification was all that was necessary to establish MCP as a foundational standard for agentic workflows, distinguishing it from protocols requiring extensive iterative documentation phases. [Source: ev-13]
  - The adoption of Google's Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol has accelerated with remarkable speed since its April 9, 2025 announcement at Google Cloud Next [ev-11], driven by a critical need for interoperability in multi-agent systems. [Source: ev-13]
  - While early launch partners included major enterprise entities like Salesforce, ServiceNow, and UiPath [ev-18], the ecosystem has rapidly expanded beyond initial hype to include tangible production deployments within Azure AI Foundry and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore as of mid-2026 [ev-18]. [Source: ev-13]
  - This rapid maturation is evidenced not merely by partnership counts but by search interest metrics; keyword volume for "a2a protocol" grew 52% quarter-over-quarter through April 2026, signaling strong market validation [ev-11]. [Source: ev-13]
  - A significant barrier to widespread adoption was the fragmentation of agent frameworks prior to A2A's release. [Source: ev-13]
  - Before this standardization, agents built on different platforms could not reliably delegate tasks without custom glue code, creating a siloed environment where vendor-specific internal messaging formats prevented collaboration [ev-20]. [Source: ev-13]
